+# The Daily Grind — moving the installed app to a new origin
+
+One shared static app is built into two deployable sites:
+
+| Build output   | Origin (from `site.config.json`) | Role                                            |
+| -------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
+| `dist/legacy`  | `legacyOrigin`                   | Where users installed from. Authorises the move. |
+| `dist/next`    | `nextOrigin`                     | The new home. Claims the legacy installation.    |
+
+```sh
+pnpm run build          # writes dist/legacy and dist/next
+pnpm run start:next     # serve dist/next  (PORT, default 11222)
+pnpm run start:legacy   # serve dist/legacy (LEGACY_PORT, default 11223)
+```
+
+Change origins only in `site.config.json`. The build derives every place they
+must agree: both manifests, the `.well-known` association file, and the runtime
+`config.js` each site loads.
+
+## What moves, and how
+
+### 1. The installation (OS shortcuts, taskbar / home-screen icon) — browser-native
+
+Web App Origin Migration lets the browser re-point an installed app to a new
+origin in place, keeping the OS-level shortcut and the app identity. It is a
+two-sided handshake that the build emits for you:
+
+* **New origin claims the old one.** `dist/next/manifest.webmanifest` has a
+  stable `id` and `migrate_from: [{ id: "<legacyOrigin>/" }]`.
+* **Old origin authorises the new one.**
+  `dist/legacy/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` contains
+  `{ "<nextOrigin>": { "allow_migration": true } }`.
+* **Old origin nudges existing installs.** `dist/legacy/manifest.webmanifest`
+  carries `migrate_to` so installed legacy apps are offered the move the next
+  time the browser checks the manifest, without waiting for a visit to the
+  new site.
+* Manifest `shortcuts` (Order, Menu, Rewards) are identical on both origins, so
+  the jump-list entries survive the move.
+
+Set `"forceMigration": true` in `site.config.json` to make the prompt
+mandatory (the browser then offers **Update** or **Remove**, never a silent
+delete).
+
+**Constraint:** both origins must be same-site (same registrable domain, same
+scheme). `app.dailygrind.com` → `order.dailygrind.com` is fine;
+`dailygrind.com` → `dailygrind.coffee` is refused by browsers, and the build
+fails early on obvious mismatches.
+
+**Support:** Chrome / Edge 149+. Firefox and Safari keep the old install. For
+them the legacy site shows a banner linking to the new origin (see below), and
+the user re-installs manually.
+
+### 2. User configuration — app-level handoff
+
+Storage is origin-bound and is **not** migrated by the browser. Preferences
+(`localStorage` key `dg:prefs`: pickup location, default milk, name on cup) are
+carried over by `app/migration.js`:
+
+1. On first launch at the new origin, the page embeds a hidden iframe of
+   `<legacyOrigin>/migrate-export.html`.
+2. That page reads the legacy origin's own (unpartitioned, because same-site)
+   storage and `postMessage`s the preferences with `targetOrigin` pinned to
+   `nextOrigin`, so no other embedder can receive them.
+3. The new origin accepts the message only if `event.source` is its iframe and
+   the sender is exactly same-origin with `legacyOrigin` (via the `Origin`
+   API, falling back to `URL` parsing where it is missing). The payload is
+   passed through `sanitizePrefs`, saved, the form is refreshed, and a toast
+   confirms the carry-over.
+4. The new origin acknowledges; the legacy origin records `exportedAt` and its
+   banner changes to "your preferences have already been moved".
+
+Rules: preferences already set on the new origin are never overwritten; a
+silent legacy (offline, decommissioned) is retried on up to three launches and
+then given up; an unexpected payload `version` is treated as "nothing to
+import".
+
+## Deployment order
+
+1. Deploy `dist/legacy` to the legacy origin first. It must serve
+   `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` (ideally as `application/json`)
+   and `/migrate-export.html` before the new origin goes live.
+2. Deploy `dist/next` to the new origin.
+3. Keep the legacy origin up for as long as you want preferences to carry
+   over; the install migration itself needs the association file reachable.
+
+Recommended headers on the legacy origin for `/migrate-export.html`:
+`Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors <nextOrigin>` and
+`X-Robots-Tag: noindex`. Not required for safety (the message is already
+origin-pinned) but a sensible second layer.
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+app/                     shared site (index.html, app.js, prefs.js, origins.js, migration.js, icons/)
+origins/legacy/          files only the legacy origin serves (migrate-export.html)
+scripts/build.mjs        emits dist/<role> with generated config.js, manifest, .well-known
+site.config.json         the single place origins and names are defined
+```
